=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables FORWARD chain on both distribution switches serving Office A — dswa1 and dswa2. Specifically, two rules were injected into the `table ip filter / chain FORWARD` on each device.

**Mechanism:** The injected ruleset creates a selective ICMP-only hole in what is otherwise a full block. Rule 1 explicitly accepts ICMP packets from 10.1.0.0/24 (Office A VLAN 10, the br0.10 SVI) destined for 10.3.0.0/24 (Office B VLAN 10). Rule 2 — evaluated immediately after — drops everything else matching the same src/dst pair. Because nftables evaluates rules in order and the chain's default policy is accept, only ICMP crosses the inter-office boundary. All TCP and UDP traffic from Office A clients is silently dropped before it ever reaches the core switches. This explains why ICMP ping to 10.3.0.100 succeeds (dswa1 shows 6 ICMP packets accepted from our own tests), but `nc` TCP connections to 10.3.0.100 time out with no RST.

**Localization:** Both dswa1 (br0.10 = 10.1.0.2/24) and dswa2 (br0.10 = 10.1.0.3/24) carry the same two-rule ACL, making this the FORWARD-plane owner of all drops. Traffic from pc1 (10.1.0.100) and pc2 (10.1.0.101) traverses br0.10 on these switches toward Office B.

**Root cause:** "Last night's change" injected a selective-ICMP ACL into the FORWARD chain on dswa1 and dswa2 that silently discards all non-ICMP traffic (TCP, UDP) from Office A (10.1.0.0/24) to Office B VLAN 10 (10.3.0.0/24), breaking application-layer connectivity between the two offices.

**Fix:** Remove the two injected rules from the FORWARD chain on both dswa1 and dswa2: `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD <handle>` for both the ICMP-accept and the blanket-drop rules. This restores full bidirectional TCP/UDP connectivity from Office A to 10.3.0.0/24.
=== END ===